<!--
    A C function that only tail-calls through a function pointer is not a
    selector stub, and the positional rule still holds at a call to it.

      tramp      mov rax,[g_fp]; mov esi,5; jmp rax             (g_fp(x, 5))
      tramp2     mov rax,[g_fp]; mov rdi,[g_x]; mov esi,1; jmp rax
                                                                (g_fp(g_x, 1))

    Both read no register on entry and leave through an indirect jump, and
    both write rsi before anything could read it.  The callers are clang -O0:
    each calls puts, which kills rsi, then divides with cqo; idiv, which
    leaves the remainder in rdx with no other reader:

      caller     tramp((a - e) / 3)
      caller2    q = (a - e) / 7; tramp2(); return q;

    rsi is definitely-not-used at both calls and rdx scores active.  The
    positional rule is what keeps the remainder off the call: a C callee
    takes its parameters in order, so a register after one the caller did
    not fill is no argument however the callee fills it.  Only an
    Objective-C selector stub (objc_msgSend$<sel>), whose convention has no
    parameter in the selector register, steps over it
    (kuna_calleefilledarg.rs, option calleefilledarg).

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  Bytes are clang
    14: tramp/tramp2 at -O2, the callers at -O0; puts is a ret stub.
-->
